*** MICROSERVICE ARCHITECTURE ***

--- Before microservice, the standard way of developing application was with a monolithic architecture, meaning that all the component of the application(the whole code) are part of a single unit, If we had an online shopping system, the user auth, product catalog, shopping cart, notification, sales component and payment would be in one codebase, everything is developed, deployed and scaled as one unit. This implies that the application must be written in one language, one tech stack, with a single runtime. Issues with this is that when a particular component/functionality fail, developers will have to rebuild, repackage and redeploy the whole code. 

-- (HOW DO WE BREAK DOWN AN APPLICATION INTO MICROSERVICE???) It is most suitable to break down microservices base on business functionality rather than technical functionalities. There should be seperation of concerns(1 service for 1 specific job).

--- MicroServices should be self contained and independent(meaning that each service must be able to be developed, deployed, and scaled seperately without any tight dependency on any other service even though they're all part of the same application[this is called LOOSE-COUPLING]). 
--- Every service have their own version, and can be debugged, scaled and maintained independent of other service.

--- Communication between microservices... since each service are built, developed and deployed seperately, there need to be a mode of communication between them. 
>>> A very common way for this service to communicate is using API calls(each service has an endpoint for which it accept request from another service), service can communicate with each other by sending http request on this endpoint(This is called SYNCHRONOUS COMMUNICATION where one service sends a request to another service and WAITS for a response). 
>>> Another common way of communication between service is using a MESSAGE BROKER(using an ASYNCHRONOUS COMMUNICATION, here the service will send a message first to the message broker for e.g rabbitMQ, and the broker will then forward the message to the destination service).
>>> Lastly, another mode of communcation is using a SERVICE MESH(here we have a helper service which takes control over the communication logic so we don't have to code the communication service)

-- API gateway handles incoming request and route them to their respective microservice.
--- The API gateway rely on an identity provider service, to handle the authentication and authorization of each request coming through the API gateway.
-- To locate a service to route an incoming request to, the API gateway consult a SERVICE REGISTRY and DISCOVERY SERVER.(microservice register with this service registry, and discover the location of other microservice through the discovery service).


