Service Discovery
--- We have been able establish a synchronous comm. with the order service and inventory service using the webclient class. 
--- Just as good as it seems, it works locally but in a MS environment we will have this services deployed on a cloud env, and on a cloud env we can not have dedicated ip addresses, everything will be dynamic(ip). Also we can have multiple instances of the inventory service for instance(inventory service deployed on a cloud is given an ip, then we create more than one instance of the inventory service subnetting the ip(same network ip different host ip)). Here with this dynamic ip nature of the inventory, the order service will lose sight of which service to call. 
--- We introduce a pattern called SERVICE DISCOVERY, this pattern is nothing but creating a server(discovery server) which will store all the info about the services(service name and service ip). When we are using a DS, our MS will first register to the DS on service boot(this will add the service info into the DS(called registry or service registry)). Once all this info is present, when our order service wants to call the inventory service it will first make a call to the DS asking where it can find the inventory service(OS be like, hey DS where can I find IS), then the DS will check its registry and return the information of the inventory service(ip). In this way we can avoid hardcoding the url of the inventory service as we did in the webclient in order service.
--- One more thing to note is that when the order service makes a request to the DS, requesting for the inventory service, the response from the DS, is stored in a local registry of the client(order service) such that if for a minion reason the DS goes down and the order service makes a request, the request won't fail yet but rather the order service will check its local registry and try using the stored inventory service info. If the response from its local registry is null(no response), then the order service will terminate(saying that the inventory service is unavailable). Let's actualize this speakings.
--- First we create a new module in our root maven project(we call the module discovery-server), this module is automatically added to the project pom xml. Next we want to add the necessary dependency for the discovery server which is called NETFLIX EUREKA(it is part of the spring cloud framework). We will go to spring.io, and add the dependency(eureka server) such that we can copy it from the pom xml or rather we can go to maven repo. After copying the dependency we will paste it in the DS pom xml file(the eureka server is coming from group id of spring framework .cloud, therefore we need to copy the dependency in the dependency management section of the spring.io pom file, we will then paste in the pom file of the root project under the dependency management section, and augment the spring cloud version). We can now create a package name(com.ShoppingMS.DiscoveryServer), then we create the main class file(DiscoverServerApplication) and then add the @SpringBootApplication minion and to make the module now a eureka server, in this main class we will add the @EnableEurekaServer minion. 
--- In addition to the minion, we will create the app.prop file in the resources folder and add some config(SEE CODE).

--- After the config, we run the DS and it runs perfectly. Now we need to register the client(services[inventory, order and product]) to the discovery server, first we will add the necessary dependency(oga copy the eureka server dependency from the DS and paste it in the services, then change the server to client) to each service pom file, then in the main file of the services we will add the @EnableEurekaClient minion(we added the @EnableDiscoveryClient since we are using spring 3.x and our cloud version is 2024.) and lastly we add some config to the app.prop file of the services(SEE CODE)
