Distributed Tracing
--- So far so real good, we have been able to implement the resiliency of the system. But there arise another issue obviously😅, its not funny okay. Now how do we track down this issues? we can examine the logs... this is cool but in production, it is not feasible due to thousands or millions of log entries. For this reason, we introduce a design pattern known as DISTRIBUTED TRACING. As the name implies, it helps us track/trace the request from the start to finish, such that if a request fails we can know when it failed, and why it failed.
--- Lets examine the flow(oh dear,, I feel good rn), now examine the request we make to place an order. This request will take its journey from the api gateway, to the order service, then to inventory service to ensure the ordered item is in stock, then back to the order service and maybe payment etc. Understand the request flow, now to be able to track the request from the api gateway to the inventory service, we need some kind of mechanism to trace this request. For this we introduce the trace ID(a unique ID which identifies a request that comes into a system), along with the trace ID, we also have a span ID(the number of trips the req. will take inside the system. We have one trip to api gateway, another to order service, then another to the inventory service), for each of this trip, we have a span ID e.g api gateway can be span 1, order service span 2, etc.
--- By using this IDs, the trace ID and the span ID we can trace the whole request lifecycle, and then also ensure there are no fault experiences.
--- To implement this, we will make use of a project inside the spring cloud called spring cloud sleuth(it is a distributed tracing framework which helps us generate the trace ID and the span IDs whenever we recieve any request to the microservices),, unfortunately this project is deprecated in spring 3. Hence we will make use of Micrometer(a set of tools that simplifies the way we measure and observe our microservices)
--- Within the micrometer tool, there is a powerful tool call observation API(a special feature within the micrometer), the micrometer gives us insight on how the code is performing for every call, execution or request. Also we need some kind of UI to visualize this information(Oh dearie.. I'm feeling real good here😋😝). For this reason, we have a tool called ZIPKIN(it is a distributed tracing system that helps gather timing data needed to troubleshoot latency problems, and other microservice related issue in the service architecture).
--- For a brief overview of micrometer observation. For any observation to happen, we need to register ObservationHandler object through an ObservationRegistry. An ObservationHandler reacts only to supported implementations of an Observation.Context and can create, for example, timers, spans, and logs by reacting to the lifecycle events of an observation such as;
>> start: Observation has been started. Happens when the Observation#start() method is called.
>> stop: Observation has been stopped. Happens when the Observation#stop() method is called.
>> error: An error occured while observing.
>> event: An event happen when observing
>> etc. 
