Low vs High Cardinality in Observability
Cardinality refers to the number of unique values a metric tag/label can have. This is crucial for observability because it directly impacts storage, performance, and costs.
Low Cardinality Keys
These have a limited, bounded set of possible values:

http.method → GET, POST, PUT, DELETE (maybe 10 values max)
service.name → user-service, order-service, payment-service
environment → dev, staging, prod
region → us-east-1, us-west-2, eu-central-1
status.code → 200, 404, 500, etc.

High Cardinality Keys
These have many unique values, often unbounded:

user.id → could be millions of unique users
request.id → UUID for each request
sql.query → each query might be slightly different
file.path → thousands of different file paths
email.address → every user's email

Why Cardinality Matters
Storage explosion: If you have 3 low-cardinality tags with 10 values each, you get 10×10×10 = 1,000 metric series. Add one high-cardinality tag with 100,000 values and you get 1 billion series.
Performance impact: High cardinality can overwhelm monitoring systems, slow down queries, and increase costs dramatically.





